<ansi fg="black-bold">.:</ansi> <ansi fg="magenta">Help for </ansi><ansi fg="command">inn</ansi>

An inn offers your company a paid room for the night, a better rest than
a roadside camp. It's a real-time stay, not game time: it doesn't touch
the world clock, and weather never spoils it.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Usage:</ansi>

  <ansi fg="command">inn</ansi> / <ansi fg="command">inn status</ansi>
  Shows the price of a room here (or your stay in progress, if you're
  already resting).

  <ansi fg="command">inn rest</ansi>
  Pays for the room and starts the stay, about a minute of real time.
  While resting you can't use ordinary exits, but you can still talk and
  use most non-movement commands. You can't take a room while
  travelling.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Cost</ansi>

  A room costs 5 gold per company member, taken from your own gold when
  you pay. A settlement that distrusts your company charges more for a
  room (<ansi fg="command">help standing</ansi> explains the tiers); one that shuns your
  company won't give you a room at all.

<ansi fg="yellow-bold">Well Rested</ansi>

  When the stay finishes, every current company member's Fatigue rises
  by 60, and you and every companion with you gain the <ansi fg="yellow">Well Rested</ansi>
  buff for 30 minutes of real time — the better rest tier, cutting your
  walking strain more than a camp's Rested does (<ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>), and
  giving you +2 speed and +2 perception besides. Well Rested always
  replaces Rested, and a camp rest afterward can't
  downgrade it back. A companion who wasn't with you when the stay
  finished still gets its share of Well Rested once it rejoins, for
  whatever time is left.

Only one room is bought per stay, for your whole company; there's
nothing to strike or break afterward.

See also: <ansi fg="command">help camp</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help standing</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help survival</ansi>, <ansi fg="command">help conditions</ansi>,
<ansi fg="command">help travel</ansi>.
